---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson 18</p>
  <h1>Weather &amp; <em>seasons</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Move beyond a list of sky words. Describe a general condition with <span class="ex">It’s rainy</span>, focus on an event happening now with <span class="ex">It’s raining</span>, read a simple forecast with clear temperature units, and discuss seasons without assuming the same pattern everywhere.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">weather noun · adjective · event</span>
    <span class="tag">current conditions · forecast · temperature</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare a day’s condition with an event happening now.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Choose the word family and time viewpoint.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Read temperature, probability, and forecast labels.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Give a fictional community weather bulletin.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Weather</span> describes conditions over a short time, such as now, today, or this week. <span class="ex">Climate</span> describes patterns observed over many years. One cold day does not prove a cold climate, and a long-term climate pattern does not tell you the exact weather at this moment.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved present continuous for live events, in with an ordinary season, and the present-continuous negative.">
    <h3>Three quick checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the camera time and the time expression. Say each complete sentence aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="raining">raining</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rainy">rainy</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in">in</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on">on</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="isn’t snowing">isn’t snowing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="doesn’t snow">doesn’t snow</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The live camera shows water falling now: It’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="raining" data-hint="A live event in progress uses the -ing form." data-fix="It’s raining now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> now.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fictional festival happens during the ordinary season: It opens <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="in" data-hint="Use in before a season when there is no this, next, or last." data-fix="It opens in winter." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> winter.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The current camera shows no falling snow: It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="isn’t snowing" data-hint="The camera describes an event not happening now, so keep be plus -ing." data-fix="It isn’t snowing now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> now.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Meet the weather words</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Store each picture with a useful grammar frame. Some labels are adjectives, while others are nouns.</p>
  <div class="figure-grid">
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/sunny.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">sunny</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/cloudy.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">cloudy</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/rainy.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">rainy</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/stormy.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">stormy</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/snowy.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">snowy</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/windy.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">windy</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/foggy.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">foggy</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/rainbow.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">a rainbow</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/lightning.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">lightning</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/fire.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">hot</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/snowflake.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">cold</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Meet four common season names</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Many calendars use four seasons: <span class="ex">spring, summer, fall</span>, and <span class="ex">winter</span>. This is one useful model, not one universal weather schedule.</p>
  <div class="figure-grid">
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/spring.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">spring</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/summer.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">summer</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/fall.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">fall</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/winter.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">winter</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice the speaker’s viewpoint</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A forecast and a live camera</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">The forecast calls Tuesday a rainy day. On the live camera, it isn’t raining at 8:00 a.m. At 8:15, water begins to fall, so now it’s raining.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which word describes Tuesday as a broader kind of day?</li>
      <li>Which form focuses on water falling at one specific moment?</li>
      <li>Can a day be called rainy even when the rain stops for a short time?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated broad weather descriptions from rain and snow events happening at an exact moment.">
    <h3>Discovery check: description or event?</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the whole context. A broader condition often uses an adjective; an event in progress uses be plus -ing.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Weather description and event choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rainy">rainy</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="raining">raining</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="snowy">snowy</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="snowing">snowing</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="cloudy">cloudy</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="shining">shining</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Showers start and stop throughout the fictional Tuesday: It’s a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rainy" data-hint="The sentence classifies the whole day, not one exact moment." data-fix="It’s a rainy day." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> day.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The camera shows water falling at 8:15: It’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="raining" data-hint="Water is falling at this exact camera time, so use the event form." data-fix="It’s raining at 8:15." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> at 8:15.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The lodge report describes a white landscape after last night’s storm, with no flakes now: It’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="snowy" data-hint="The report describes the place’s condition, not falling snow now." data-fix="It’s snowy at the lodge." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> at the lodge.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The live camera shows flakes falling now: It’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="snowing" data-hint="Falling flakes create an event in progress." data-fix="It’s snowing now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> now.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The sky is covered with clouds, but no rain is falling: It’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="cloudy" data-hint="Cloudy is the adjective for a sky with many clouds." data-fix="It’s cloudy, but it isn’t raining." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>, but it isn’t raining.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The real subject is named, and bright light is coming from it now: The sun is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="shining" data-hint="With the real subject the sun, use is plus the event verb shining." data-fix="The sun is shining now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> now.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: noun, adjective, or event</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Weather words form families, but they do not all use the same pattern. Choose the grammar from the message, not from the picture alone.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>weather idea</th><th>noun</th><th>adjective</th><th>event now</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>rain</td><td><span class="ex">a chance of rain</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s rainy.</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s raining.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>snow</td><td><span class="ex">some snow</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s snowy.</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s snowing.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>wind</td><td><span class="ex">The wind is strong.</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s windy.</span></td><td><span class="ex">The wind is blowing.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>sun</td><td><span class="ex">the sun</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s sunny.</span></td><td><span class="ex">The sun is shining.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>cloud</td><td><span class="ex">a cloud</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s cloudy.</span></td><td><span class="contraction">no ordinary “It’s clouding” pattern</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>fog</td><td><span class="ex">some fog</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s foggy.</span></td><td><span class="contraction">usually describe the condition</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <h3><span class="ex">It</span> in a weather sentence does not point to an object</h3>
    <p>In <span class="ex">It’s cold</span> and <span class="ex">It’s raining</span>, <span class="ex">it</span> is a required weather subject. It does not refer back to a thing. Do not normally drop it: say <span class="ex">It’s windy</span>, not only <span class="ex">Is windy</span>.</p>
    <p>When a real subject is named, use that subject: <span class="ex">The sun is shining</span>, <span class="ex">The wind is blowing</span>, and <span class="ex">The clouds are moving</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Do not add -ing to every weather noun</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">It’s raining</span> and <span class="ex">It’s snowing</span> are common weather-event patterns. English does not build every event by attaching <span class="ex">-ing</span> to the picture label. Say <span class="ex">The sun is shining</span>, not <span class="ex">It’s sunning</span>; say <span class="ex">It’s windy</span> or <span class="ex">The wind is blowing</span>, not <span class="ex">It’s winding</span>.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Lightning</span> and <span class="ex">thunder</span> are normally uncountable in basic weather use: <span class="ex">There is some lightning and thunder.</span> <span class="ex">A rainbow</span> and <span class="ex">a storm</span> are countable nouns.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected the rain and wind word families plus uncountable lightning from the grammar around each gap.">
    <h3>Drill 1: choose the family member</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Weather word family choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rain">rain</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rainy">rainy</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="raining">raining</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="wind">wind</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="windy">windy</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="lightning">lightning</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The forecast gives a probability: There is a 60 percent chance of <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rain" data-hint="After chance of, use the weather noun rain." data-fix="There is a 60 percent chance of rain." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The report classifies the whole day: It will be a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rainy" data-hint="Before the noun day, use the adjective rainy." data-fix="It will be a rainy day." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> day.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The camera shows water falling now: It’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="raining" data-hint="The live event uses the -ing form." data-fix="It’s raining now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> now.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The noun is the subject of the sentence: The <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="wind" data-hint="After the, use the noun wind." data-fix="The wind is strong." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> is strong.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The weather adjective follows it is: It’s very <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="windy" data-hint="After it’s very, use the adjective windy." data-fix="It’s very windy." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The storm report uses the uncountable noun: There is some <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="lightning" data-hint="Some introduces the uncountable weather noun lightning." data-fix="There is some lightning in the distance." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> in the distance.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Ask for the information you need</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>information wanted</th><th>natural question</th><th>possible answer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>broad current description</td><td><span class="ex">What’s the weather like?</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s cool and cloudy.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>same broad description</td><td><span class="ex">How’s the weather?</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s warm and sunny.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>one current event</td><td><span class="ex">Is it raining?</span></td><td><span class="ex">No, it isn’t.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>a number and unit</td><td><span class="ex">What’s the temperature?</span></td><td><span class="ex">It’s 72 degrees Fahrenheit.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>expected later conditions</td><td><span class="ex">What’s the forecast for Friday?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Cloudy, with a high of 68°F.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">What’s X like?</span> asks for a description. The word <span class="ex">like</span> does not mean enjoy in this question. Do not combine the two question frames as <span class="ex">How is the weather like?</span></p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched broad weather, one live event, a temperature, and a later forecast to four different questions.">
    <h3>Drill 2: choose the question from the answer needed</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Weather question choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="What’s the weather like?">What’s the weather like?</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Is it raining?">Is it raining?</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="What’s the temperature?">What’s the temperature?</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="What’s the forecast for Friday?">What’s the forecast for Friday?</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The caller wants a broad description of current conditions: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="What’s the weather like?" data-hint="A broad current description answers What’s the weather like?" data-fix="What’s the weather like? It’s cool and cloudy." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The caller only needs to know whether water is falling now: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Is it raining?" data-hint="A yes-or-no question with raining checks one current event." data-fix="Is it raining? No, it isn’t." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The caller expects the answer “72 degrees Fahrenheit”: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="What’s the temperature?" data-hint="A number with Fahrenheit or Celsius answers a temperature question." data-fix="What’s the temperature? It’s 72 degrees Fahrenheit." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The caller needs expected conditions for a later day: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="What’s the forecast for Friday?" data-hint="Forecast asks about expected later conditions." data-fix="What’s the forecast for Friday? Cloudy, with a high of 68°F." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Read temperature and probability carefully</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Always make the unit clear across countries</h3>
    <p>U.S. local forecasts commonly use <span class="ex">Fahrenheit</span>; most countries use <span class="ex">Celsius</span>. In an international conversation, include the unit: <span class="ex">72 degrees Fahrenheit</span> or <span class="ex">about 22 degrees Celsius</span>. Do not assume that <span class="ex">35 degrees</span> creates the same temperature picture for every listener.</p>
    <p>Use singular after one and plural after other numbers: <span class="ex">1 degree</span>, <span class="ex">72 degrees</span>. <span class="ex">High</span> is the warmest temperature expected in the forecast period; <span class="ex">low</span> is the coolest.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>A chance is not a promise</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">A 60 percent chance of rain</span> is a probability estimate for a place and forecast period. It means rain is possible, not certain. It does not simply mean rain for 60 percent of the day. Forecast services may define and calculate the number in more detail.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Seasons depend on place and system</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The four-season model does not produce the same month or weather picture everywhere.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Under the four-season model, January is winter in the Northern Hemisphere and summer in the Southern Hemisphere.</li>
    <li>Places near the equator may have less seasonal temperature change. Some local calendars focus on wet and dry seasons instead.</li>
    <li>A season name does not guarantee one condition. A winter day can be sunny, and a summer day can be rainy.</li>
  </ul>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>time frame</th><th>preposition pattern</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>season in general</td><td><span class="ex">in + season</span></td><td><span class="ex">The program opens in spring.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>common U.S. alternative</td><td><span class="ex">in the + season</span></td><td><span class="ex">The program opens in the spring.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>this, next, or last</td><td><span class="ex">no in</span></td><td><span class="ex">It opens next spring.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">American English commonly uses <span class="ex">fall</span> for the season between summer and winter. <span class="ex">Autumn</span> is also understood and is especially common in British English.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: short frames, clear message words</h3>
    <p>In relaxed speech, <span class="ex">it is</span> becomes one-syllable <span class="ex">it’s</span>. Keep the weather message prominent: <span class="ex">It’s <strong>RAIN</strong>ing</span>, <span class="ex">It’s <strong>WIND</strong>y</span>, and <span class="ex">It’s <strong>SEV</strong>enty-two degrees</span>.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Weather</span> begins with a voiced <span class="ex">th</span> and sounds like <span class="ex"><strong>WEATH</strong>-er</span> in U.S. English. <span class="ex">Whether</span> often sounds the same but has a different spelling and meaning. <span class="ex">What’s the weather like?</span> links as <span class="ex">What’s-the <strong>WEATH</strong>er-like?</span></p>
    <p>Common U.S. pronunciations include <span class="ex"><strong>TEM</strong>-pruh-cher</span> for <span class="ex">temperature</span>, <span class="ex"><strong>FAIR</strong>-en-height</span> for <span class="ex">Fahrenheit</span>, and <span class="ex"><strong>SEL</strong>-see-us</span> for <span class="ex">Celsius</span>. Careful speech may keep more sounds in <span class="ex">temperature</span>.</p>
    <p>Keep word-family differences audible: <span class="ex">rain</span> has one syllable, while <span class="ex"><strong>RAIN</strong>-y</span> and <span class="ex"><strong>RAIN</strong>-ing</span> have two. The noun <span class="ex">wind</span> in weather uses the short vowel in <span class="ex">win</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read condition, event, forecast-label, probability, and hemisphere listening check">
