  <h2>Use it safely and flexibly</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real location, weather, climate, home conditions, clothing, health, mobility, work, travel, schedule, plans, finances, family, or access needs. Use the fictional forecast cards and service plans, invent details, or share personal information only when you freely choose. For real severe weather, follow official local alerts; this language lesson is not safety guidance.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Separate the times:</b> Use one fictional card to state current conditions, one live event that is or is not happening now, and one later forecast.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Change the word family:</b> Move from a noun phrase such as <span class="ex">a chance of rain</span> to <span class="ex">a rainy day</span> and then to <span class="ex">It’s raining now</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Read the numbers responsibly:</b> Give a current temperature, forecast high, forecast low, and chance of rain. Include Fahrenheit or Celsius every time.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Compare fictional places:</b> Use supplied Northern Hemisphere, Southern Hemisphere, wet-season, and dry-season cards. Report only what each card states; do not guess climate from a country name.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Final production: give a fictional community weather bulletin for 45–60 seconds.</b> Include a broad current condition, one event happening or not happening now, a later forecast, a temperature with its unit, a rain probability interpreted as possible rather than certain, one place-specific season fact, the supplied service change, and one follow-up weather question.</div>

